[38;5;231m⏺[39m I renamed [38;5;153muserId[39m to [38;5;153muser_id[39m in the [38;5;153mGET /users[39m response. It's a one-line change in [38;5;153mroutes/users.js:6[39m and nothing else
  in the repo uses the old name. I haven't committed it.

  [1mThis breaks the API for current clients.[0m The README lists three consumers of [38;5;153mGET /users[39m: the web app, the iOS app (3.2
  and later), and the nightly partner export. It says field names "are part of the contract; changes go through a
  versioned endpoint." If this ships as is, any of those clients reading [38;5;153muserId[39m will get [38;5;153mundefined[39m. The iOS app is the
  hardest to fix, because older installs will keep reading the old name.

  If you want to stay within that policy, there are two options:
  - [1mVersioned endpoint:[0m keep [38;5;153m/users[39m unchanged and add something like [38;5;153m/v2/users[39m that returns [38;5;153muser_id[39m.
  - [1mTransition period:[0m return both [38;5;153muserId[39m and [38;5;153muser_id[39m until the clients have moved over, then remove [38;5;153muserId[39m.

  I can switch to either of these, or leave the plain rename if the clients are already being updated together with this
  change.

